libqrencode-ng NEWS - Overview of changes
=========================================

Version 5.0.0 (2026-01-08)
--------------------------

This is the first release of libqrencode-ng, a community fork of libqrencode
by Kentaro Fukuchi (https://github.com/fukuchi/libqrencode).

* Project renamed to libqrencode-ng for parallel install with upstream:
  * Library is installed as libqrencode-ng.so.5
  * Public header is installed to <prefix>/include/qrencode-ng/qrencode.h
  * pkg-config module name is "libqrencode-ng" (link with -lqrencode-ng)
  * Command-line tool is installed as "qrencode-ng"
  * CMake package is "qrencode-ng" with target qrencode-ng::qrencode-ng

* Build system overhaul:
  * Migrated to CMake-only (Autotools support removed entirely)
  * Minimum CMake version is 3.16
  * C11 is now the required language standard
  * GCC and Clang are the only supported compilers
  * Strict warning flags (-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic) are enabled by default
  * Optional sanitizer (ASan / UBSan), code coverage and gprof builds via
    QRENCODE_ENABLE_ASAN, QRENCODE_ENABLE_UBSAN, QRENCODE_ENABLE_COVERAGE,
    QRENCODE_ENABLE_GPROF

* Platform support narrowed to Linux and BSD. Windows- and macOS-specific
  code paths have been removed; the SDL-based viewer has been dropped.

* Hardening:
  * ANSI / ANSI256 / ANSIUTF8 / ANSI256UTF8 output buffers in the CLI tool
    are now sized correctly, fixing potential overflows on large inputs.
  * Source modernized to use C11 idioms and stdbool.

* Command-line tool ("qrencode-ng"):
  * Added "--eci=N" to embed an ECI header (assignment number 0-999999).
  * Added "--fnc1-first" to add the FNC1 first-position mode indicator.
  * Added "--fnc1-second=AI" to add the FNC1 second-position mode indicator
    with the application identifier.

* Bug fixes inherited from upstream:
  * Fixed level check failure in QRinput_new2() on platforms exposing the
    same edge case (originally reported on Windows).
  * Compile-time warnings cleaned up across qrspec.c and other modules.

Release Note:
The library SOVERSION is 5, intentionally bumped from upstream's 4 because
this fork is not ABI-compatible (changes to internal types and the C11
migration). The library, header, pkg-config module, CMake package and CLI
have all been renamed with an "-ng" suffix so this package can be installed
alongside upstream libqrencode on the same system. Consumers should update
their build system to use the new names:

    cc $(pkg-config --cflags libqrencode-ng) myprog.c \
       $(pkg-config --libs libqrencode-ng)

and update their includes:

    #include <qrencode-ng/qrencode.h>

The C API itself is otherwise source-compatible with upstream libqrencode
4.1.1.

================================================================================
The release notes below cover upstream libqrencode (versions 1.0.0 through
4.1.1) before the fork.
================================================================================


Version 4.1.1 (2020.9.28)
-------------------------
* Some minor bugs in Micro QR Code generation have been fixed.
  * The data capacity calculations are now correct. These bugs probably did not
    affect the Micro QR Code generation.

Version 4.1.0 (2020.8.29)
-------------------------
* Command line tool "qrencode" has been improved:
	* New option "--inline" has been added. (Thanks to @jp-bennett)
	* New option "--strict-version" has been added.
	* UTF8 mode now supports ANSI256 color. (Thanks to András Veres-
	  Szentkirályi)
* Micro QR Code no longer requires to specify the version number.
* 'make check' allows to run the test programs. (Thanks to Jan Tojnar)
* Some compile time warnings have been fixed.
* Various CMake support improvements. (Thanks to @mgorny and @sdf5)
* Some minor bug fixes. (Thanks to Lonnie Abelbeck and Frédéric Wang)
* Some documentation/manpage improvements. (Thanks to Dan Jacobson)
* Some performance improvements. (Thanks to @4061N and Mika Lindqvist)

Release Note:
The internal representation of the output code has been slightly changed -
the second bit from LSB side now represents; 1:ECC bit / 0:data bit.
This change is only for debug purposes and does not affect user applications.

